# Patch: Kimchi runtime and UI integrations for pi-coding-agent 0.85.1
#
# Tracking: tools/bash.js bounded abort/timeout termination: earendil-works/pi#10929
#   (upstream issue with a minimal reproduction of the never-settling abort and
#   the proposed shared termination; an upstream PR submitting the equivalent
#   source change and regression tests is planned against it). The /model
#   selector and remaining runtime/UI hunks are still publication pending; no
#   upstream issue or PR has been filed for those.
# Changes:
#   - Kimchi CLI/provider defaults, extension-triggered prompt handling, forced
#     compaction, model selection, extension UI helpers, and compact visuals.
#   - Session-log durability for externalized tool results: the
#     session manager persists the FULL tool-result content even when an
#     extension replaced the in-memory message content with a compact lazy
#     marker (`[kimchi-ext:tool]` JSON handle pointing at a disk-backed
#     mirror). `_serializableEntry` resolves the original content from the
#     marker's own file reference at serialization time, so the JSONL stays
#     the complete, self-contained resume source while the live entry keeps
#     the marker. Marker reads are path-confined: tool markers must point at
#     this process's mirror (globalThis.__kimchiToolOutputFile, published by
#     kimchi's store) and log markers at a live session file (bounded LRU
#     registry, 16 files: closed sessions drop out of the window). Spoofed
#     markers in tool output are never resolved. Tool markers restore the
#     block-array shape. `_rewriteFile` restores marker entries from their
#     own persisted line (mirror-free) and refreshes in-memory log-marker
#     offsets to the rewritten layout. The most recently active session file
#     is published as globalThis.__kimchiSessionFile for the kimchi-side
#     mirror-loss hydration fallback. Removal: when pi exposes a
#     content-persistence hook or lets extensions control persisted content.
#   - Compaction-history display: SessionManager gains
#     buildDisplayEntries() (full leaf path, pre-compaction entries kept) and
#     the interactive mode renders it at the three transcript call sites
#     (initial render, rebuild, post-compaction) via a capability probe, so
#     the TUI keeps the complete history visible after compaction. The model
#     context source (buildContextEntries) is untouched. Removal: when
#     upstream exposes a display-entries hook.
#   - Compaction-history residency: after a compaction commits,
#     pre-cut message entries' in-memory content is evicted to compact
#     `[kimchi-ext:log]` markers (offsets into the session JSONL); markers
#     resolve transparently at the sessionEntryToContextMessages read seam
#     with a bounded LRU, and the serializer keeps the log canonical on
#     rewrites. Resumed sessions evict at open (_loadEntries) instead of
#     loading the full history into memory. The session log remains the
#     durable source of truth.
#   - Auth-storage lock-free reads: reloadFromStorageAsync reads auth.json
#     directly instead of taking the exclusive lock (orphaned crash-time locks
#     caused 30s startup stalls). The branch returns the bare credential map —
#     withLockAsync's contract; returning the callback's `{ result }` shape
#     instead made the first read after an external revision change resolve
#     undefined ("API key auth failed for provider kimchi-dev"). Reads retry
#     torn partial files with a short backoff (upstream modify() is non-atomic
#     truncate-then-writeFileSync; kimchi's syncPiAuth writes atomically, so
#     partials are only observable during an upstream modify). Persistently
#     corrupt files resolve undefined; the next revision change re-arms the
#     reload. Regression tests: src/pi-auth-storage.test.ts.
#     Removal: upstream lock-free reads with bare-map return + torn-read
#     tolerance, atomic upstream modify(), or kimchi retaking the read lock.
#   - Auth-storage lock-free reads handle the 0-byte truncate window: a read
#     sampling a non-atomic writer's open(O_TRUNC) moment sees an empty file,
#     which parseStorageData() maps to {} WITHOUT throwing — the read would
#     silently resolve "no credentials" and request auth would hard-fail
#     ("API key auth failed for provider …" from the models.json
#     $KIMCHI_API_KEY fallback) instead of retrying. The hunk now treats an
#     empty read as a torn write and retries it inside the backoff window; a
#     missing file (content undefined) remains a valid no-credentials state.
#     Regression test: pi-auth-storage.test.ts "0-byte tear".
  - Startup resource diagnostics: skill conflicts render as a single dim
#     summary line ("[N skill conflicts] Some skills were found in multiple
#     directories. <key> to see the full list.") that expands to the full
#     collision listing via the shared app.tools.expand toggle, instead of a
#     full warning-colored listing on every session start. Prompt conflicts
#     keep upstream's rendering (rare, and genuinely actionable when they
#     happen). (Upstream candidate.)
#   - Extension setModel accepts { persist } and threads it to AgentSession's
#     ModelMutationOptions, restoring the persistence channel upstream made
#     opt-in. setThinkingLevel is deliberately NOT threaded: Kimchi's only
#     caller (src/extensions/tags.ts, set_phase) is tool-driven and must stay
#     session-only, so the plumbing would have no caller. (Upstream candidate.)
#   - /model selector renders a capability table: MODEL | PROVIDER | CONTEXT |
#     VISION | DESCRIPTION columns, all left-aligned (content starts under its
#     column name; the ✓/✗ VISION cell pads to the 6-wide header so
#     DESCRIPTION content lines up). The DESCRIPTION column combines the
#     endpoint-provided model description (registry:
#     process.__kimchiModelDescriptions, populated by src/models.ts from
#     /v1/models/metadata, persisted through models.json) with the
#     "Default for new sessions." annotation, replacing the old `· default`
#     badge. The virtual multi-model row inherits its orchestrator model's
#     stats/description and is annotated "[Deprecated]" —
#     a literal mirroring MULTI_MODEL_DEPRECATION_LABEL in
#     src/extensions/auto-model/constants.ts, which the patched dist cannot
#     import (ACP sources the constant; rendered-text tests in
#     src/model-selector-table.test.ts and tests/e2e/tui pin both sides to
#     the same string). Layout width reads tui.terminal.columns (pi-tui's
#     Terminal exposes `columns`, not `cols`); the last column is clipped,
#     never padded, so the header cannot wrap onto a phantom blank line, and
#     rows total exactly the terminal width. On narrow terminals the
#     description column truncates first, then the provider column, the model
#     id last. The shared renderer lives in src/model-selector-table.ts;
#     removal criteria for this hunk are in the Removal note below.
#   - exportToJsonl is awaited: src/cli.ts replaces the prototype with an async
#     wrapper (trace IDs + awaited PII redaction, fail-closed). Upstream's method
#     is sync, so this hunk looks redundant - it is not. Dropping it prints
#     [object Promise] and returns before redaction completes.
#   - tools/bash.js timeout pipe-cleanup lives in createLocalShellOperations, so
#     it covers the PowerShell tool too (intentional).
#   - tools/bash.js bounded termination in createLocalShellOperations, shared
#     by abort AND timeout (covers the PowerShell tool too, intentionally):
#     kill the command's process group, then destroy the local inherited
#     stdout/stderr pipe ends after a 500 ms grace so a detached descendant
#     that keeps writing through them cannot re-arm waitForChildProcess's
#     idle timer forever (a cancelled execution would otherwise stay pending
#     until the descendant's own exit). The cleanup timer is scheduled at most
#     once when abort and timeout overlap, and cleared in finally on earlier
#     settlement; abort/timeout error messages and precedence and the
#     already-aborted handling are unchanged. Tracking: earendil-works/pi#10929.
#   Two fixes for third-party npm extensions (e.g. plannotator):
#   - Type2 ReferenceError: Bun's bundler renames Google GenAI's `Type` enum to
#     `Type2` (collision with typebox's `Type`); the pi-ai compat module re-exports
#     `Type` from typebox, but the bundled getter references the broken `Type2`
#     binding. A Proxy overrides `.Type` with the typebox namespace directly.
#     Only observable in the compiled binary.
#   - Flag conflict crash: when an npm extension registers a flag that kimchi
#     already provides (e.g. `--plan`), the conflict is downgraded from a hard
#     error to a warning (kimchi built-in extensions load first and win).
# Security (project trust): extends hasTrustRequiringProjectResources
# (dist/core/trust-manager.js) with Kimchi's project-local .kimchi/ and .claude/
# resources (config, permissions, hooks, skills, agents, memory, tags, plans,
# ferments, MCP servers, pi-agent skills) so a cloned repo that ships them triggers the trust
# prompt instead of being auto-trusted. The list is checked in cwd AND its
# ancestors (matching the ancestor-walking skills/tags readers), stopping at the
# user's home (a user resource) and the OS temp directory (not a project
# tree — a stray .kimchi/.claude created there by any tool must not gate
# unrelated temp-dir sessions). The trust prompt and the untrusted warning
# (dist/core/project-trust.js, dist/modes/interactive/interactive-mode.js)
# mention those resources and speak as Kimchi, not pi.
# Careful: pasteToEditor is NOT patched here - src/paste-to-editor-patch.ts owns
# Removal: remove the selector adapter when the pinned Pi release provides equivalent rendering extensibility or native capability columns. Remove the tools/bash.js bounded-termination hunk when the pinned Pi release ships equivalent bounded abort cleanup (upstream: earendil-works/pi#10929); keep the Kimchi regression tests in src/extensions/bash-background/ after removal. Remove other runtime/UI hunks when upstream provides equivalent APIs or Kimchi no longer needs the branded behavior. Remove trust hunks when Pi provides native trust-scan extensibility or Kimchi adopts Pi's config-directory layout. Keep trust resources synchronized with `TRUST_REQUIRING_PROJECT_RESOURCES` in `src/project-scope-trust.ts` until then.
# History: patches/CHANGELOG.md.
#
